McFarlin succeeds Cohen
Larry McFarlin, former standout Texas-El Paso baseball player, has been named the head baseball coach according
to Athletic Director Jim Bowden,
McFarlin, only the second baseball coach in the school's history since it became an intercollegiate sport, fills the va-
cancy left when Andy Cohen resigned early this summer.
“Andy had served as our coach from the inception of the program in 1963 and is well known and respected throughout the
baseball world. Obviously it is difficult to replace a man of coach Cohen's stature, but we feel the appointment of Larry
McFarlin as his replacement is a step in that direction.”
McFARLIN WAS an outstanding player
for the Miners in the mid-60s and still holds a number of Miner records including number of home runs and number of
runs batted in for a season. After completing his eligibility at UTEP in 1967, he played professional baseball for one year
with the California Angels system before returning to El Paso to finish his degree and teach,
“T am elated on the selection of Larry as the new head coach,” Cohen said, “In fact, I'm proud of the fact that one of my
former players was chosen to succeed me, He was always a leader with the unique qualities of being a hard worker and a de-
dicated player with a great attitude, And with these same ingredients as a coach, I'm sure Larry will be successful here at UTEP.”
Cohen has decided to remain at the University as a consultant to aid the baseball program during the transition period.
“Larry brings with him an outstanding record, not only as a player but also as an enthusiastic worker with young people
and as an ambassador for the University athletic program. His youth and enthusiasm coupled with Andy Cohen’s age and
experience should give us the ingredients necessary to build UTEP’s baseball program to the same level of competitiveness
we are striving for in the other sports," Bowden said.
In addition to his duties as baseball coach, McFarlin will also aid the department in promotion and recruiting for all sports.


