signals from the third base coaching box.
It is the only sport which spans this tumultuous 20th century, Major league baseball, in particular, has been a perpetual calendar, graciously
ticking off the years, marking time.
Almost every present-day manager in baseball can be traced back to two men who were teaching the game in weaning of this century — John 
McGraw and Branch Rickey.
McGraw managed the Giants for more than 30 years and Rickey built two organizations, the Cardinals and 
the Dodgers. McGraw begat Casey Stengel, who tutored Billy Martin.
Gene Mauch, Dick Williams, Tom Lasorda and Danny Ozark are eee of Rickey’s Dodger system. Jim Fregosi played under Bill ey, who
played under Mel Ott, who played under, yep, John J. McGraw.
Leonard Koppett, a renowned sports writer for the New York Times, once pointed out that the current in-
crease of base-stealing in the majors was coming from teams whose managers were disciples of either
McGraw or Rickey.
But Koppett had to search to find a link with those men to Chuck Tanner, whose teams at Oakland and Pittsburgh were, and are, baseball's best
thieves on the basepaths. But he concluded: “Do you know who Chuck Tanner's closest friend is, who managed him in the minors and who was,
